# Summary
Historical summary for commit `2f977e8`: the new pending direct-PUT command
mechanism was not integrated with object delete operations. Pending direct PUTs
were replayed by later reservation calls, but deletes neither applied nor
conflicted with pending object metadata commands before modifying primary
metadata.

Direct PUT metadata was staged as a pending metadata command and applied to all
acting metadata nodes. If application failed after at least one non-primary node
had applied the command, the command remained pending. Because the acting set was
sorted so the primary applied last, such a failure could leave the primary
unaware of the new object while replicas and the in-memory pending-command table
remembered it. The only object path that drained these pending commands was
reserve_put_object_generation. Current-object and version delete operations
routed directly to the primary node and read/deleted the primary metadata without
checking pending_metadata_commands. Therefore, after a failed direct PUT left a
pending CommitDirectPutObject, a delete could succeed against the primary state,
and a later PUT reservation in the same bucket would apply the stale pending
commit and restore the previously failed object. This violated object deletion
and conditional-write integrity under transient metadata-node failures.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm metadata command application can partially succeed on non-primary nodes before failing on the primary.
- [x] Confirm direct PUT leaves `CommitDirectPutObject` pending after a partial apply failure and does not clear it except on zero-node failure.
- [x] Confirm delete-current/delete-specific object paths route directly to primary metadata and do not inspect or drain `pending_metadata_commands`.
- [x] Dynamically reproduce that delete succeeds while the pending direct-PUT commit remains and the primary object metadata is removed.
- [x] Dynamically reproduce that a later generation reservation drains the stale pending direct-PUT commit and makes the previously failed object live again on all acting nodes.
## Report
Validated against commit `2f977e8` with a targeted Rust unit-test PoC plus a non-interactive LLDB trace. The relevant code supports the suspected flow: `crates/storage/src/cluster/request_ops.rs:374-410` applies metadata commands to acting nodes with the primary sorted last; `crates/storage/src/cluster.rs:1063-1090` stores a pending `CommitDirectPutObject` and only removes it on zero-node failure, so a failure at the primary after replica application leaves the command pending; `crates/storage/src/cluster.rs:748-766` is the only object path observed draining pending metadata commands during `reserve_put_object_generation`; `crates/storage/src/cluster/request_ops.rs:1832-1839` routes current-object delete directly to the object metadata primary; and `crates/storage/src/node/object_delete_ops.rs:340-380` reads/deletes primary PG metadata without checking pending commands.

Dynamic reproduction: I added a targeted test `delete_does_not_drain_pending_direct_put_commit_and_later_reserve_restores_it` in `crates/storage/src/cluster/local.rs`. The test creates a 3-node local cluster, commits an old object, reserves/writes a new direct PUT, injects a failure on the object-PG primary for `CommitDirectPutObject` so the command remains pending after non-primary nodes apply it, then calls `delete_current_object_if`. The test asserts: pending command exists after the failed direct PUT; primary still has the old generation; delete succeeds and deletes the old generation; pending command is still present after delete; primary then reports `ObjectNotFound`; a later `reserve_put_object_generation` drains the stale pending command; and all acting nodes subsequently show the failed direct PUT generation as live again. Command output: `test cluster::local::tests::delete_does_not_drain_pending_direct_put_commit_and_later_reserve_restores_it ... ok`.

Crash/valgrind/debugger attempts: the PoC is a logic-integrity bug and does not crash; running the debug test profile produced no crash but did reproduce the invalid state transition. `valgrind` was not installed (`bash: valgrind: command not found`). I then used LLDB non-interactively against the debug test binary. LLDB hit `StorageCluster::delete_current_object_if` at `request_ops.rs:1838`, called from the PoC at `local.rs:2796`, confirming the primary-only delete path. It also hit `StorageCluster::reserve_put_object_generation` at `cluster.rs:748`, called from the PoC at `local.rs:2827`, confirming the later reservation enters the pending-command drain path after the delete.

